.TH OMN 1 "2026-09" "Omniscient" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
omn \- one vocabulary for system observability
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B omn
.I source
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ target ]
.RI [ "\-\- program [args]" ]
.br
.B omn
.B \-f
.I session
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B omn list
.RI [ family | source ]
.br
.B omn validate
.B \-f
.I session
.br
.B omn formats
.br
.B omn latency
.B \-\-objective
.I objective
.RI [ "\-\-runs N" ]
.RB [ \-\-pid
.IR pid " | " "\-\- program [args]" ]
.br
.B omn saturate
.B \-\-objective
.I objective
.RB [ \-\-pid
.IR pid " | " "\-\- program [args]" ]
.br
.B omn diff
.I before after
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omn
describes a measurement along five axes and refuses the combinations that cannot
mean anything, rather than answering them with a number that means something
else.
.PP
It speaks to
.BR perf_event_open (2),
.BR bpf (2),
procfs, sysfs and netlink directly. It does not wrap
.BR perf (1),
.B bpftrace
or
.BR bcc ,
so there is no second output format to parse and no second clock in the middle
of the pipeline.
.SS The five axes
.TP
.B source
What to measure, as
.IR family . metric :
.BR io.latency ", " cpu.instructions ", " net.tcp.rtt .
.BR "omn list" " enumerates them."
.TP
.B target
What to observe:
.BR \-\-pid ", " \-\-tid ", " \-\-cgroup ", " \-\-cpu ", " \-\-iface ", "
.BR \-\-container ", " \-\-system ", or a program to launch after " \-\- .
.TP
.B mode
How to observe:
.BR count ", " sample@997hz ", " trace ", " poll@100ms ", " snapshot .
.TP
.B emit
What comes out:
.BR raw ", " hist ", " rate ", " topk ", " stack ", " timeline ", " summary .
.TP
.B keys
Correlation keys to attach:
.BR req ", " task ", " proc ", " file ", " sock ", " blk ", " mem ", " gpu ", "
.BR cgroup ", " iface .
.PP
A bare
.BI "omn " source
is short for
.BI "omn trace " source .
.SS What it refuses
The validator runs before anything is attached. It rejects a target a source
cannot carry, a mode it does not support, an emit that mode cannot produce, a
correlation key it does not carry, a filter field it does not expose, and an
output format that cannot express the requested emit. Each refusal says why and
what the source does accept.
.PP
Returning a machine-wide value while presenting it as per-process attribution is
the worst bug this tool could have, so the combination fails rather than
degrading.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "trace " source
Build a request, resolve it against this machine, and run it. The default
command, which may be omitted.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIfamily\fR|\fIsource\fR]
Project the catalog. With no argument, every source; with a family, that family;
with a source, that entry in full, including every backend, what each one
requires and how each requirement was probed.
.IP
The listing answers three different questions in three columns.
.B HERE
is what this machine offers, probed now.
.B COLLECTOR
is what this build can measure, which is not the same question.
.B COST
is the cheapest backend in a chain, and
.B DET
is reproducibility between runs \(em never bit-exactness.
.IP
.B \-\-verify\-attach
answers every raw\-tracepoint requirement by attaching to it rather than by
reading the kernel's BTF. Those are not the same question: BTF says the kernel
defines a tracepoint, and the attach is what a run will actually do. They have
never yet disagreed, which the suite checks rather than assumes, and asking the
expensive one of every entry costs about a second \(em so it is a switch and not
the default. Reach for it on a machine where a backend probes available and then
refuses once a target is running.
.TP
.B validate \fB\-f\fI session
Check a session against the catalog without touching the machine. Machine
independent, so it answers for a host other than this one.
.TP
.B formats
The input and output formats, which emits each output format can carry, and
which encoders exist yet.
.TP
.B latency \-\-objective \fIobjective\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIprogram\fR
Why is this slow. Thread state analysis, asked as a question: one task's wall
clock decomposed into on a cpu, runnable and blocked, the blocked time split by
the task state each block recorded, and the state that dominates named with
what covers it \(em a pthread mutex, a futex, the stack a block began from, the
hottest function. See
.BR TRIAGE .
.IP
.BI \-\-objective " latency" "\fR|\fP" tail
is required. \fBlatency\fR decomposes the median run; \fBtail\fR decomposes
the slowest run against the median and names the state it spent more time in.
.BI \-\-runs " N"
runs a launched program \fIN\fR times (default 1; the tail needs 3 or more).
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
decomposes a process already running over \fB\-\-duration\fR, which it then
requires; its whole is the window rather than a lifetime, and it has no tail.
.BI \-\-hz " HZ"
is the profile's rate (default 997), whose period is the on-cpu row's
resolution.
.TP
.B saturate \-\-objective \fIobjective\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIprogram\fR
What does this queue for. The USE method \(em utilization, saturation and
errors per resource: cpu, disk, lock, memory \(em with Little's law as its
arithmetic.
.BI \-\-objective " throughput" "\fR|\fP" tail "\fR|\fP" energy
is required. See
.BR TRIAGE .
.TP
.B diff \fIbefore after\fR
Two profiles and a subtraction. Each is a run's output directory (written with
.B \-o
and
.B \-\-format json
or
.BR folded ),
its
.IR results.json ,
or a folded file. Names the functions whose own cost moved beyond the noise a
sample count has, compared in samples at one rate and in time at two.
.B \-\-format folded
writes the three-column
.I "stack before after"
file differential flame graphs are drawn from;
.BI \-\-top " N"
bounds each table.
.IP
The three triage commands take
.B \-\-format
text, json or yaml, and
.B \-\-dump\-session
prints the session the question is asked through and exits: an ordinary
session, which
.B omn \-f
runs to print the tables the answer is read from.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Target
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID" "\fR, " \-\-tid " TID"
An existing process or thread. Repeatable as a comma-separated list.
.TP
.BI \-\-cgroup " PATH"
A cgroup v2 path.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " SET"
Cores, written the way the kernel writes them:
.BR 0\-3,8 .
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " NAME"
A network interface.
.TP
.BI \-\-container " NAME"
A container, resolved to a cgroup plus a pid namespace.
.TP
.B \-\-system
The whole machine.
.TP
.BI \-\- " program " [ args ]
Launch the program and measure it. The program is held at a gate until every
collector is attached, so the measurement starts before its first instruction,
and the run ends when it does.
.SS Measurement
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mode " MODE"
.BR count ", " trace ", " snapshot ", " sample@\fIFREQ\fR ", " poll@\fIINTERVAL\fR.
A frequency is
.BR 997hz " or " 1khz ;
an interval is
.BR 100ms ", " 1s ", " 2m .
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-emit " EMIT"
Comma-separated.
.BR hist " takes " :log2 " or " :linear: \fIWIDTH\fR,
and
.BR topk " takes " : \fIN\fR.
Asking for two emits costs one collection.
.TP
.BI \-k ", " \-\-keys " KEY"
Correlation keys to attach, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter " EXPR"
A predicate:
.RB ` "bytes > 4KiB && rwbs == 'R'" '.
Supports
.BR == " " != " " < " " <= " " > " " >= " " =~ " and " in ,
grouped with
.BR && ", " || ", " ! " and parentheses."
Integers take size suffixes
.RB ( 4KiB ", " 4KB )
and duration suffixes
.RB ( 5ms ).
.IP
.BR =~
is a substring test rather than a regular expression:
.RB ` "comm =~ 'python'" '
keeps
.BR python3 .
A pattern that can only be a regex \(em a character class, a group, an escape,
or an anchor \(em is refused rather than matched literally.
.IP
The resolver pushes a predicate into the kernel where the backend allows it and
evaluates it here otherwise, and says which it did. A userspace filter is paid
for in full before it removes anything, and what it removed is reported beside
the losses.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-duration " DURATION"
How long to observe. Required unless a program is launched or the mode is
.BR snapshot .
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-budget " PERCENT"
An overhead ceiling, as
.BR 2% ,
read as a percentage of the observed workload's own CPU time. The run weighs
itself ten times a second against that; over the ceiling it moves to a cheaper
backend in the chain, or, where there is none, stops. Either way the reason is
in
.I stopped_because
and on standard error. Every run reports what it cost, budget or no budget.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " MODE"
.BR fp ", " dwarf ", " lbr " or " none .
.B dwarf
copies the stack on every sample: far more volume and cost than frame pointers.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
.BR text " (default), " json ", " yaml ", " toml ", " ndjson ", " folded ", "
.BR speedscope ", " pprof ", " chrome .
A result format only accepts the emits it can express;
.B omn formats
says which, and which encoders exist.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-out " DIR"
Write the results and the resolved request into a directory. The resolved
request is always written as JSON whatever the results format is: results
without it are numbers whose provenance is gone.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-request
Print the request and how it resolves on this machine, then exit without
measuring.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-resolved
Print the resolved request alone \(em the record to keep beside the numbers.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resolve
Do not consult this machine. For writing down a session meant to run somewhere
else, where what this machine can do says nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
.BR auto " (default), " always " or " never .
Colour is an accelerator and never the carrier of a fact: everything it
highlights is also written in words.
.SS Session files
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-file " PATH"
Read the session from a file, or from standard input with
.BR \- .
YAML, JSON and TOML are accepted, inferred from the extension.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-format " FORMAT"
.BR yaml ", " json " or " toml ,
when the extension does not say or the input is standard input.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " force:ID"
Pin a backend instead of resolving one. For the comparison bench: a result
obtained this way is not the result the tool would have produced, and the record
says so. A session naming one source several times, each pinned to a different
backend, is the self-benchmark: one target, one window, and a table of what each
backend cost and whether they agreed.
.TP
.BI \-\-catalog " DIR"
Load the catalog from a directory instead of the one compiled in. For iterating
on entries without rebuilding.
.SH SESSION FILES
A session file describes exactly what a command line does, and
.B \-\-dump\-request
prints one. The two surfaces cannot express different things, which is what
keeps them from drifting apart.
.PP
.RS
.EX
name: which allocation actually cost memory
duration: 30s
format: text

requests:
  \- label: ranges
    source: mem.heap.alloc
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [summary]

  \- label: pages
    source: mem.faults
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [rate]

joins:
  \- id: pages\-of\-allocation
    from: mem.heap.alloc
    to: mem.faults
    via: mem
    match: interval
    opens: "op == 'alloc'"
    closes: "op == 'free'"
    window: 60s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every request in one session is collected over one window. Running them one
after another would measure several different moments and present them as one,
and a join across that would correlate a program with its own past. A session
has at most one \fIworkload\fR target \(em \fBlaunch\fR, \fBpid\fR, \fBtid\fR,
\fBcgroup\fR or \fBcontainer\fR \(em and any number of \fImachine\fR targets
beside it (\fBsystem\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBiface\fR): that is the shape of every
confounder check, where the machine's state is read beside the program it might
explain. Only the workload target decides when the run ends.
.SS Joins
A join is declared beside its sources and matches their events by a key.
.TP
.B interval
An event is credited to the state whose range it falls in: an allocation's
pages to the allocation. Opened and closed by predicates.
.TP
.B eq
Both sides carry the same key. Which of several open states of one key an
event goes to is decided by recency, and is reported as \fBwithin_key:
TEMPORAL\fR beside the \fBKEY\fR, because recency is the clock and not the key.
.TP
.B scoped_eq
The same, on a key whose values are recycled: a request pointer is scoped by
the moment it was opened, \fBblk:\fIptr\fB@\fIopen_ts\fR.
.TP
.B wakeup
A task's run is credited to the wakeup that began it. Refused before the run
onto a \fBdrained\fR source, whose events are sums of blocks.
.TP
.B between
A polled total's change between two readings is credited to the states that
opened or closed in that interval. \fBTEMPORAL\fR by construction.
.SS Decompositions
A \fBdecompose:\fR block splits a whole into parts, per key:
.PP
.RS
.EX
decompose:
  \- id: where\-the\-time\-went
    whole: lifetime
    parts:
      \- {state: on\-cpu,   from: cpu.profile,   value: samples}
      \- {state: runnable, from: sched.runqlat, value: total_ns}
      \- {state: blocked,  from: sched.offcpu,  value: total_ns, less: queued_ns}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A part reads \fIvalue\fR off each event of its source \(em an attribute, or
\fBsamples\fR for a sampled source, which is the count times the period \(em
and may take \fBless:\fR an attribute to subtract, \fBfilter:\fR a predicate
choosing which events it reads, \fBreading: level\fR|\fBdelta\fR, required for a
polled source, and \fBnames:\fR an attribute whose most frequent value the row
names beside its amount, with how many of its events agreed. \fBby:\fR is the
key each table is read per (\fBtask\fR unless said), and \fBtop:\fR how many
tables to print.
.PP
The whole is \fBwindow\fR (the run, from its start to the last drain: an upper
bound), \fBlifetime\fR (the launched program, from the gate to the reap: exact
at the start, bracketed at the exit, and refused where nothing was launched),
or a measurement read off a source. Each row says what kind of number it is \(em
sampled, polled, traced, counted \(em and its resolution. The residue is
\fIwhole \- parts\fR, signed and never rounded away: negative is something
counted twice, positive is a state no row covers, and one inside its own
resolution is the decomposition holding.
.SH EVIDENCE
Every correlated result carries the grounds it was obtained on, because a
correlation backed by a propagated identity and one backed by two things
happening at the same time are both "correlations" and only one is evidence.
.TP
.B IDENTITY
The downstream event carries an identity the upstream one propagated.
.TP
.B KEY
Both sides carry the same typed key.
.TP
.B WAKEUP
A wakeup chain links them: the only join that crosses a block.
.TP
.B TEMPORAL
They overlapped in time. A hypothesis, nothing more. What \fBbetween\fR
produces, and what \fBwithin_key\fR says of an \fBeq\fR join's choice between
states.
.SH TRIAGE
.BR "omn latency" ", " "omn saturate" " and " "omn diff"
sit above the engine and call it like any other user: each writes a session,
runs it through the same calls
.B omn \-f
makes, and reads the answer off the results. What they compute themselves \(em
a share, a Little's-law product, a difference of two profiles \(em is
arithmetic on numbers the engine reported, with every row's evidence,
resolution and shortfall carried through. A value marked \fB>=\fR comes from a
lossy run and is a lower bound: what a stalled machine fails to record is the
slow part.
.SS The objective
"What is this limited by" has no answer until someone says \fIfor what\fR, and a
triage question without \fB\-\-objective\fR is refused with the list rather
than defaulted.
.TP
.B latency
How long a typical run takes: the median run, decomposed.
.TP
.B tail
How long the slowest runs take. For \fBomn latency\fR, what the slowest of
\fB\-\-runs\fR spent that the median did not \(em the maximum of \fIN\fR,
which is not a percentile below a hundred runs, and says so. For \fBomn
saturate\fR, the resource whose individual waits have the longest 99th
percentile, read off each source's histogram and known to its log2 bucket.
.TP
.B throughput
How much work gets done per second: the resource the work queues for. Only
\fBomn saturate\fR answers it; a wall clock does not.
.TP
.B energy
What burns the energy this machine meters. Only \fBomn saturate\fR answers
it: the package's energy is credited to the workload by its share of the
machine's busy cpu time, which is an estimate and is marked \fBTEMPORAL\fR, and
the disk's and the network's energy are named as unmetered rather than free.
.PP
Each method refuses what it cannot answer and names the one that can:
\fBomn latency \-\-objective throughput\fR points at \fBomn saturate\fR, and
\fBomn saturate \-\-objective latency\fR at \fBomn latency\fR.
.SS Verdicts
\fBomn latency\fR names the largest state only when nothing unread could
overtake it: not the next state, not a row the run did not supply, not the
residue and the rows' resolution. Otherwise it says undecided, and why. It
answers for one task \(em a launched program's main thread, or the process
asked for \(em and lists the others. A sleeping state is named by the lock
source that covers more than half of it: \fBsched.mutex\fR (\fIlock\fR), then
\fBsched.futex\fR (\fIfutex\fR), else \fIsleep\fR with the stack it began
from; \fIuninterruptible\fR is \fIdisk\fR.
.PP
\fBomn saturate\fR reads, per resource, the workload's side: cpus busy with it
and tasks waiting for a cpu; requests in flight at the device, tasks blocked on
it, and requests that failed; a mutex's hold time and its waiters; its
cgroup's memory stall share. Every mean count is Little's \fIL\fR, the arrival
rate times the mean wait over the window \(em the sum of the waits over
\fIT\fR, exact over a traced source \(em and printed term by term.
For throughput it names the longest queue behind something another task holds,
a cpu or a lock, when one reaches a tenth of a waiter on average; below that,
nothing is saturated and the workload is limited by its own concurrency, which
is the answer for a program waiting on one synchronous write at a time.
.PP
\fBomn diff\fR compares absolute costs, not shares: when one function doubles,
every other function's share falls without any of them changing. A function
is the difference when its own (leaf) cost moved beyond three standard
deviations of Poisson noise, \fI|b \- a| / sqrt(a + b)\fR; the inclusive table
shows what carried it. A folded file carries no loss accounting and no rate,
and the answer says it could not check either.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The measurement ran, or the question was answered.
.TP
.B 1
This run cannot happen here: this machine cannot serve the request, or
something failed while running. The request itself is valid and will run on a
machine that has what it needs.
.TP
.B 2
The request was refused: it cannot mean what it asks for. A flag that does not
parse, an axis a source does not carry, a triage question without an objective
or with one its method does not answer for, a tail asked of one run.
.TP
.B 3
The request is valid and nothing in this build collects it yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OMN_CATALOG_DIR
Load the catalog from this directory instead of the one compiled in.
.TP
.B OMN_PRELOAD_LIB
Path to the heap shim, when it is not beside the binary.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and non-empty, or
.B TERM=dumb
, disables colour.
.SH PRIVILEGES
The eBPF backends need
.B CAP_BPF
and
.BR CAP_PERFMON ,
and several sysfs counters need root. An unprivileged run is refused with the
capability that is missing, never resolved onto a lesser backend that answers a
different question.
.PP
File capabilities do not survive a relink, so every rebuild drops them and
nothing announces it: the tool reports fewer sources as available, which reads
like the machine changed rather than the binary.
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep $(which omn)
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I catalog/*.yaml
The vocabulary, as data. Adding a source is an entry here, not code.
.TP
.I examples/*.yml
One session per question worth asking. Each opens with the question it answers
and what to expect.
.IR examples/triage\-*.yml " are the sessions the triage commands ask through."

.IR GUIDE.md " explains what each one shows and how to run one, a family, or"
all of them.
.TP
.I <out>/resolved.json
The backend selected, every requirement and how it was probed, what was
degraded, and the machine's kernel, governor, THP mode, SMT state and
clocksource. Two measurements taken through different backends are not
comparable, and this is what says which was used.
.TP
.I <out>/cost.json
What the measurement itself cost: the CPU time this tool burned, the CPU time
the target burned over the same window and where that number was read, what
weighing the run cost, and the band the backend declared set beside the
percentage it actually came to.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Resident memory of a program, as it runs:
.EX
omn mem.rss \-\-emit timeline \-\-mode poll@100ms \-\- ./build.sh
.EE
.TP
Which allocation actually cost memory:
.EX
omn \-f examples/allocation\-cost.yml
.EE
.TP
Round\-trip time of every TCP connection this machine holds:
.EX
omn net.tcp.rtt \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-d 30s \-e timeline
.EE
.TP
Instructions retired by a program, the most reproducible number in the catalog:
.EX
omn cpu.instructions \-m count \-e summary \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
Block I/O over 4 KiB, as a histogram:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-system \-m trace \-e hist \-\-filter "bytes > 4KiB"
.EE
.TP
What this machine can serve, and what this build can measure:
.EX
omn list
.EE
.TP
Why a program is slow, for its median run:
.EX
omn latency \-\-objective latency \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
What makes its slowest run of twenty slow:
.EX
omn latency \-\-objective tail \-\-runs 20 \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
What a program queues for, and what it costs in energy:
.EX
omn saturate \-\-objective throughput \-\- ./build.sh
omn saturate \-\-objective energy \-\- ./build.sh
.EE
.TP
Which function a change made slower:
.EX
omn cpu.profile \-m sample@997hz \-e stack \-\-stack fp \-\-format json \-o before \-\- ./old
omn cpu.profile \-m sample@997hz \-e stack \-\-stack fp \-\-format json \-o after \-\- ./new
omn diff before after
.EE
.TP
Write a session down for a machine other than this one:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-pid 1 \-m trace \-e hist \-\-no\-resolve \-\-dump\-request \-\-format yaml
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR perf_event_open (2),
.BR bpf (2),
.BR proc (5),
.BR sysfs (5),
.BR capabilities (7)
.PP
.IR GUIDE.md " for the demonstrations and what each shows,"
.IR CATALOG.md " for the vocabulary, " STATE.md " for what works today,"
.IR DECISIONS.md " for what is settled and why,"
.IR docs/scenarios/ " for what is worth measuring in each family."
